.TH SSSD-SMARTCARD 8 "2026-10-11" "sssd-smartcard 0.2.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
sssd-smartcard \- generate, validate and deploy SSSD Smart Card / PIV configuration
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sssd-smartcard
.RI [ global-options ]
.I group command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sssd-smartcard
configures authentication of Linux users with X.509 certificates held on a
Smart Card or PIV token, through SSSD.
.PP
Its central operation is to read a real certificate, work out which
certificate\-to\-user mapping mechanisms that certificate can actually support,
rank them, and emit a complete profile together with the
.B sssd.conf
section to go with it. The PKI that issued the certificate is irrelevant:
Microsoft AD CS, EJBCA, Blinky and any other X.509 PKI all feed the same
mechanisms, and only the fields present in the certificate differ.
.PP
The private key on the card is never read, and no PIN is ever requested,
stored or forwarded. Every PKCS#11 call passes
.BR \-\-type\ cert .
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
These may be given before or after the subcommand.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine readable output.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable colour. The
.B NO_COLOR
environment variable has the same effect.
.TP
.BI \-\-sssd\-version " X.Y.Z"
Target this SSSD version instead of the one detected on this host. Options the
target version does not have cause a refusal rather than a silent downgrade.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Location of
.BR sssd.conf .
Default
.IR /etc/sssd/sssd.conf .
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS cert inspect | cert autoconfig
Show everything about a certificate that SSSD cares about \(em subject, issuer,
serial, the full Subject Alternative Name including the Microsoft UPN and any
PKINIT principal, extended key usage, key usage, SKI, AKI, validity, the Active
Directory SID security extension, and the advertised CRL and OCSP endpoints
\(em then propose a configuration.
.PP
The certificate comes from
.BI \-\-file " PATH"
(PEM or DER),
.BI \-\-base64 " B64"
,
.B \-\-stdin
, or
.B \-\-from\-card
.
.TP
.BI \-\-mapping " TYPE"
Force a mapping mechanism instead of the highest ranked one.
.TP
.BI \-\-domain " DOMAIN"
Override the account domain inferred from the certificate.
.TP
.BI \-\-sssd\-domain " DOMAIN"
The domain this host is joined to, which owns the
.RI [ certmap/... ]
section. Read from
.B sssd.conf
when not given.
.TP
.BI \-\-save\-profile " PATH"
Write the generated profile.
.TP
.B \-\-with\-revocation
Enable revocation checking in the generated profile.
.TP
.B \-\-require\-smartcard
Generate a profile that demands Smart Card only login.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Only inspect; do not generate a configuration.
.SS card detect
Enumerate PC/SC readers, PKCS#11 modules, token slots and the certificate
objects on the card, and mark the one that looks like the authentication
certificate.
.SS profile list | show | validate
List the known profiles, show one with its rendered rule, or check profiles for
errors against the target SSSD version.
.SS mapping types | test
.B mapping types
lists the available mapping mechanisms with their options and version
requirements.
.PP
.B mapping test
runs a certificate through a profile and reports, term by term, which parts of
the matching rule passed, what the mapping rule expands to, the resulting LDAP
filter, and whether SSSD can resolve the identity. Two engines run: a local
one that works anywhere, and
.BR "sssctl cert\-eval\-rule" ,
which is libsss_certmap itself and whose verdict wins. A disagreement between
them is reported rather than hidden.
.SS domain test | list
Show the primary and trusted domains, their online state, and optionally
resolve a user with
.BR getent ,
.B id
and
.BR "sssctl user\-checks" .
.SS krb5 status | test
.B krb5 status
shows the credential cache and whether it holds a
.B krbtgt
ticket.
.B krb5 test
checks the realm, KDC discovery and reachability, and the certificate's
fitness for PKINIT. With
.B \-\-pkinit
it runs
.B kinit
with the card into a throwaway credential cache, which is the only way to
prove that the KDC really issues a ticket. The PIN is typed into
.B kinit
directly.
.SS ca show | import
Inspect CA certificates or the trust bundle, or merge certificates into the
bundle named by
.BR pam_cert_db_path .
The existing bundle is read and merged, never replaced; duplicates are skipped
by fingerprint and a backup is taken first.
.SS config render | diff | validate | apply
Print the configuration fragment, diff it against what is on disk, validate it,
or apply it.
.B apply
is a dry run unless
.B \-\-yes
is given; it backs everything up, writes
.B sssd.conf
atomically at mode 0600, runs
.BR "sssctl config\-check" ,
restarts SSSD, and rolls every touched file back on any failure.
.SS pam check
Report on the PAM stack read\-only. This command never writes.
.SS doctor
Run every diagnostic in dependency order: host, SSSD, identity and trusts, PAM,
CA trust, Kerberos and the Smart Card stack.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An expected error, or a check that failed.
.TP
.B 3
The target SSSD version lacks a required feature.
.TP
.B 4
A required external helper is not installed.
.TP
.B 5
The operation only makes sense on the SSSD host itself.
.TP
.B 6
Configuration could not be applied; a rollback may have happened.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/sssd/sssd.conf
Written by
.BR "config apply" ,
always at mode 0600.
.TP
.I /etc/sssd/pki/sssd_auth_ca_db.pem
Default CA trust bundle, the documented default of
.BR pam_cert_db_path .
.TP
.I /etc/sssd-smartcard/profiles/
Profiles written by the administrator. Searched before the packaged examples.
.TP
.I /usr/share/sssd-smartcard/profiles/
Example profiles shipped by the package.
.TP
.I /var/lib/sssd-smartcard/backups/
Timestamped backups taken before any change.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SSSD_SMARTCARD_PROFILES
Additional profile directory, searched first.
.TP
.B SSSD_SMARTCARD_PKCS11_MODULE
PKCS#11 module to use when
.B \-\-module
is not given.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colour output.
.SH EXAMPLES
Read the card and propose a configuration:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sssd\-smartcard cert inspect \-\-from\-card
.fi
.RE
.PP
Derive and save a profile, then prove it against the same certificate:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sssd\-smartcard cert autoconfig \-\-from\-card \-\-save\-profile /etc/sssd\-smartcard/profiles/AD\-PIV\-UPN.yaml
sssd\-smartcard mapping test AD\-PIV\-UPN \-\-from\-card
.fi
.RE
.PP
Review and apply:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sssd\-smartcard config diff AD\-PIV\-UPN
sssd\-smartcard config apply AD\-PIV\-UPN \-\-yes
.fi
.RE
.SH CAVEATS
The local matching engine reads
.B &&
as binding tighter than
.BR || .
.BR sss\-certmap (5)
documents both operators but neither precedence nor any grouping syntax, so
confirm anything unusual with
.BR "sssctl cert\-eval\-rule" .
Distinguished name rendering for the
.B !ad_x500
family of conversions is likewise a local approximation of libsss_certmap, good
enough to preview a rule offline.
.PP
SSSD reads a certificate revocation list from a local PEM file
.RB ( crl_file= );
it does not download CRL distribution points. Something else has to refresh
that file.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sssd\-smartcard\-setup (8),
.BR sssd.conf (5),
.BR sss\-certmap (5),
.BR sssd\-ad (5),
.BR sssctl (8),
.BR pam_sss (8),
.BR kinit (1)
.PP
.I /usr/share/doc/sssd-smartcard/sssd-reference.md
records every SSSD option this program emits together with the documentation it
was taken from.
